1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,611
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll ask what
it means to add infinitely many numbers.

2
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:11,939
A sequence is an ordered list: a sub one,
a sub two, a sub three, and so on.

3
00:00:12,389 --> 00:00:15,951
One over n gives one, one half, one third,
one fourth.

4
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,919
A sequence converges if its terms approach
a single number.

5
00:00:21,369 --> 00:00:27,813
One over n converges to zero. Negative one
to the n bounces between negative one and

6
00:00:27,813 --> 00:00:29,347
one, so it diverges.

7
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:35,992
A series is the sum of a sequence's terms:
a one plus a two plus a three, forever.

8
00:00:36,442 --> 00:00:38,981
To make sense of it, we look at partial
sums.

9
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:44,537
The nth partial sum, S sub n, adds the
first n terms.

10
00:00:44,987 --> 00:00:50,159
For one half plus one fourth plus one
eighth and so on, the partial sums are one

11
00:00:50,159 --> 00:00:53,521
half, three fourths, seven eighths,
approaching one.

12
00:00:54,667 --> 00:00:59,573
A series converges if its partial sums
converge. Its sum is that limit.

13
00:01:00,023 --> 00:01:05,303
Otherwise the series diverges. One plus
one plus one keeps growing, so it

14
00:01:05,303 --> 00:01:05,954
diverges.

15
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,473
Your turn. Does the sequence n over n plus
one converge? To what?

16
00:01:11,923 --> 00:01:13,459
Pause the video and try it.

17
00:01:13,909 --> 00:01:14,891
Yes, to one.

18
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:21,835
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll study
the series we can always sum exactly:

19
00:01:21,835 --> 00:01:23,116
geometric series.

20
00:01:24,233 --> 00:01:30,335
A geometric series is a plus a r plus a r
squared and so on, where each term is r

21
00:01:30,335 --> 00:01:31,465
times the last.

22
00:01:31,915 --> 00:01:33,622
r is the common ratio.

23
00:01:34,766 --> 00:01:40,590
It converges when the absolute value of r
is less than one, and diverges otherwise.

24
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:46,821
With r equals two, the terms grow; with r
equals negative one, the partial sums

25
00:01:46,821 --> 00:01:47,334
bounce.

26
00:01:48,466 --> 00:01:52,733
When it converges, the sum is the first
term over one minus r.

27
00:01:53,183 --> 00:01:58,937
Three plus three halves plus three fourths
and so on has sum three over one minus one

28
00:01:58,937 --> 00:02:00,223
half, which is six.

29
00:02:01,366 --> 00:02:04,950
If the series starts later, use the actual
first term.

30
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:10,828
The sum from n equals two to infinity of
one third to the n has first term one

31
00:02:10,828 --> 00:02:15,491
ninth, so the sum is one ninth over two
thirds, which is one sixth.

32
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:22,510
Repeating decimals are geometric. Point
seven two seven two repeating is seventy

33
00:02:22,510 --> 00:02:25,742
two hundredths over one minus one
hundredth.

34
00:02:26,192 --> 00:02:29,414
That's seventy two over ninety nine, or
eight elevenths.

35
00:02:30,533 --> 00:02:35,760
Your turn. Find the sum of four minus two
plus one minus one half and so on.

36
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:37,746
Pause the video and try it.

37
00:02:38,196 --> 00:02:43,614
r is negative one half, so the sum is four
over three halves, which is eight thirds.

38
00:02:44,733 --> 00:02:49,597
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll learn
the quickest test for divergence.

39
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:54,104
If a series converges, its terms must go
to zero.

40
00:02:54,554 --> 00:03:01,188
So if the limit of a sub n isn't zero, the
series diverges. That's the nth term test.

41
00:03:02,333 --> 00:03:07,538
The sum of n over two n plus one: the
terms approach one half, not zero.

42
00:03:07,988 --> 00:03:09,226
So it diverges.

43
00:03:10,366 --> 00:03:13,652
If the terms do go to zero, the test tells
you nothing.

44
00:03:14,102 --> 00:03:16,320
The series might converge or diverge.

45
00:03:17,466 --> 00:03:23,155
The harmonic series, one plus one half
plus one third and so on, has terms going

46
00:03:23,155 --> 00:03:24,933
to zero, but it diverges.

47
00:03:25,383 --> 00:03:30,837
Group terms: one half, then one third plus
one fourth is more than one half, then the

48
00:03:30,837 --> 00:03:33,916
next four terms are more than one half,
forever.

49
00:03:35,066 --> 00:03:40,928
Always check the nth term test first. It's
fast, and it can rule out convergence

50
00:03:40,928 --> 00:03:41,808
immediately.

51
00:03:42,258 --> 00:03:45,372
If the terms go to zero, move on to
another test.

52
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,340
Your turn. Does the sum of cosine of one
over n converge?

53
00:03:50,790 --> 00:03:52,326
Pause the video and try it.

54
00:03:52,776 --> 00:03:58,152
The terms approach cosine zero, which is
one. Not zero, so it diverges.

55
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:03,588
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll compare
a series to an integral.

56
00:04:04,733 --> 00:04:10,061
If f is positive, continuous and
decreasing, and a sub n equals f of n,

57
00:04:10,061 --> 00:04:15,913
then the sum of a sub n and the integral
from one to infinity of f either both

58
00:04:15,913 --> 00:04:17,789
converge or both diverge.

59
00:04:18,239 --> 00:04:21,546
The rectangles of the series hug the area
under the curve.

60
00:04:22,667 --> 00:04:25,269
Check all three conditions before using
it.

61
00:04:25,719 --> 00:04:30,391
The integral's value is not the series'
sum, only a guide to convergence.

62
00:04:31,533 --> 00:04:37,570
The sum of one over n squared plus one:
the integral from one to infinity of one

63
00:04:37,570 --> 00:04:43,456
over x squared plus one is arctangent,
which goes from pi over four to pi over

64
00:04:43,456 --> 00:04:43,757
two.

65
00:04:44,207 --> 00:04:47,365
The integral converges, so the series
converges.

66
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:55,134
A p-series is the sum of one over n to the
p. It converges when p is greater than one

67
00:04:55,134 --> 00:04:57,865
and diverges when p is at most one.

68
00:04:58,315 --> 00:05:01,707
That comes from the integral test with the
p-integral rule.

69
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:09,441
The sum of one over n squared converges.
The sum of one over root n diverges, since

70
00:05:09,441 --> 00:05:10,556
p is one half.

71
00:05:11,006 --> 00:05:15,763
The harmonic series is the p-series with p
equals one: it diverges.

72
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:21,423
Your turn. Does the sum of one over n to
the one point one converge?

73
00:05:21,873 --> 00:05:23,409
Pause the video and try it.

74
00:05:23,859 --> 00:05:28,851
Yes. It's a p-series with p equals one
point one, which is greater than one.

75
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,723
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll decide
convergence by comparing to series we

76
00:05:35,723 --> 00:05:36,677
already know.

77
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:43,748
Direct comparison: for positive terms, if
a sub n is at most b sub n and the sum of

78
00:05:43,748 --> 00:05:46,184
b converges, so does the sum of a.

79
00:05:46,634 --> 00:05:52,266
If a sub n is at least b sub n and the sum
of b diverges, so does the sum of a.

80
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:59,288
The sum of one over n squared plus three:
each term is less than one over n squared.

81
00:05:59,738 --> 00:06:04,133
Since the p-series with p equals two
converges, this one does too.

82
00:06:05,267 --> 00:06:11,629
Limit comparison: if the limit of a sub n
over b sub n is a positive finite number,

83
00:06:11,629 --> 00:06:13,928
both series do the same thing.

84
00:06:14,378 --> 00:06:17,237
It's easier to use when the inequality is
messy.

85
00:06:18,367 --> 00:06:24,729
Compare to the dominant terms. For n plus
one over n cubed minus two, compare to n

86
00:06:24,729 --> 00:06:27,988
over n cubed, which is one over n squared.

87
00:06:28,438 --> 00:06:32,257
The ratio of the two goes to one, so the
series converges.

88
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:37,731
The sum of one over two n minus one:
compare to one over n.

89
00:06:38,181 --> 00:06:44,080
The ratio goes to one half, positive and
finite. The harmonic series diverges, so

90
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:45,391
this one does too.

91
00:06:46,533 --> 00:06:50,992
Your turn. Does the sum of one over three
to the n plus one converge?

92
00:06:51,442 --> 00:06:52,978
Pause the video and try it.

93
00:06:53,428 --> 00:06:59,669
Each term is less than one over three to
the n, a convergent geometric series. So

94
00:06:59,669 --> 00:06:59,977
yes.

95
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:06,519
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll study
series whose terms alternate in sign.

96
00:07:07,667 --> 00:07:13,970
An alternating series looks like b one
minus b two plus b three minus and so on,

97
00:07:13,970 --> 00:07:15,624
with each b positive.

98
00:07:16,074 --> 00:07:17,653
The partial sums zigzag.

99
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:24,227
The alternating series test: if the b's
decrease and approach zero, the series

100
00:07:24,227 --> 00:07:24,923
converges.

101
00:07:25,373 --> 00:07:29,063
Each step overshoots less than the last,
closing in on the sum.

102
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:36,319
The alternating harmonic series, one minus
one half plus one third minus one fourth,

103
00:07:36,319 --> 00:07:37,048
converges.

104
00:07:37,498 --> 00:07:41,594
Its sum is ln two, even though the
harmonic series diverges.

105
00:07:42,733 --> 00:07:48,784
The error after stopping at the nth term
is at most the next term, b sub n plus

106
00:07:48,784 --> 00:07:49,091
one.

107
00:07:49,541 --> 00:07:53,210
And the error has the same sign as that
first omitted term.

108
00:07:54,333 --> 00:08:00,093
How many terms of the alternating harmonic
series give an error under point zero one?

109
00:08:00,543 --> 00:08:06,367
We need one over n plus one less than
point zero one, so n at least one hundred.

110
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:13,160
Your turn. Estimate the error if you use
four terms of one minus one fourth plus

111
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:15,991
one ninth minus one sixteenth and so on.

112
00:08:16,441 --> 00:08:17,977
Pause the video and try it.

113
00:08:18,427 --> 00:08:23,248
The next term is one twenty fifth, so the
error is at most point zero four.

114
00:08:24,367 --> 00:08:29,587
Next time, we'll learn the ratio test. If
this helped, subscribe to School Math,

115
00:08:29,587 --> 00:08:34,351
Explained, and turn on notifications so
you don't miss it. See you there.

